, M.C., F. Mauxion, and B. S&#x000e9;raphin. <year>2001</year>. The yeast POP2 gene encodes a nuclease involved in mRNA deadenylation. <source>Nucleic Acids Res.</source> <volume>29</volume>:<fpage>2448</fpage>&#x02013;2455.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11410650</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><citation citation-type="journal">Dunckley, T., and R. Parker. <year>1999</year>. The DCP2 protein is required for mRNA decapping in <italic>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</italic> and contains a functional MutT motif. <source>EMBO J.</source> <volume>18</volume>:<fpage>5411</fpage>&#x02013;5422.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10508173</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib25"><citation citation-type="journal">Eystathioy, T., E.K. Chan, S.A. Tenenbaum, J.D. Keene, K. Griffith, and M.J. Fritzler. <year>2002</year>. A phosphorylated cytoplasmic autoantigen, GW182, associates with a unique population of human mRNAs within novel cytoplasmic speckles. <source>Mol. Biol. Cell.</source> <volume>13</volume>:<fpage>1338</fpage>&#x02013;1351.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11950943</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib26"><citation citation-type="journal">Eystathioy, T., A. Jakymiw, E.K.L. Chan, B. S&#x000e9;raphin, N. Cougot, and M.J. Fritzler. <year>2003</year>. The GW182 protein colocalizes with mRNA degradation associated proteins hDcp1and hLSm4 in cytoplasmic GW bodies. <source>RNA</source>. <volume>9</volume>:<fpage>1171</fpage>&#x02013;1173.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">13130130</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib27"><citation citation-type="journal">Fischer, N., and K. Weis. <year>2002</year>. The DEAD box protein Dhh1 stimulates the decapping enzyme Dcp1. <source>EMBO J.</source> <volume>21</volume>:<fpage>2788</fpage>&#x02013;2797.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12032091</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib28"><citation citation-type="journal">F&#x000f6;rch, P., O. Puig, N. Kedersha, C. Martinez, S. Granneman, B. S&#x000e9;raphin, P. Anderson, and J. Valc&#x000e1;rcel. <year>2000</year>. The apoptosis-promoting factor TIA-1 is a regulator of alternative pre-mRNA splicing. <source>Mol. Cell.</source> <volume>6</volume>:<fpage>1089</fpage>&#x02013;1098.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11106748</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib29"><citation citation-type="journal">Frischmeyer, P.A., A. van Hoof, K. O'Donnell, A.L. Guerrerio, R. Parker, and H.C. Dietz. <year>2002</year>. An mRNA surveillance mechanism that eliminates transcripts lacking termination codons. <source>Science.</source> <volume>295</volume>:<fpage>2258</fpage>&#x02013;2261.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11910109</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib30"><citation citation-type="journal">Fromont-Racine, M., A.E. Mayes, A. Brunet-Simon, J.C. Rain, A. Colley, I. Dix, L. Decourty, N. Joly, F. Ricard, J.D. Beggs, and P. Legrain. <year>2000</year>. Genome-wide protein interaction screens reveal functional networks involving Sm-like proteins. <source>Yeast.</source> <volume>17</volume>:<fpage>95</fpage>&#x02013;110.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10900456</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib31"><citation citation-type="journal">He, F., S.W. Peltz, J.L. Donahue, M. Rosbash, and A. Jacobson. <year>1993</year>. Stabilization and ribosome association of unspliced pre-mRNAs in a yeast upf1- mutant. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volume>90</volume>:<fpage>7034</fpage>&#x02013;7038.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8346213</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib32"><citation citation-type="journal">Iborra, F.J., D.A. Jackson, and P.R. Cook. <year>2001</year>. Coupled transcription and translation within nuclei of mammalian cells. <source>Science.</source> <volume>293</volume>:<fpage>1139</fpage>&#x02013;1142.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11423616</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib33"><citation citation-type="journal">Ingelfinger, D., D.J. Arndt-Jovin, R. Luhrmann, and T. Achsel. <year>2002</year>. The human LSm1-7 proteins colocalize with the mRNA-degrading enzymes Dcp1/2 and Xrnl in distinct cytoplasmic foci. <so